    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "When studying adolescents, language and development have a recursive relationship where language promotes development and development is expressed through language. Specifically, in identity formation, which I define as the social positioning of self and others, adolescents’ internal identities only enter the social world through discourse. To study adolescents’ identity formation through language, I turned to an ideal case: four Brazilian immigrant bi/multilingual adolescent girls living within the same Brazilian diasporic community who largely attend the same school and church in Southwest Florida, the state with the highest concentration of Brazilians in the U.S. and restrictive immigration and educational policies at play. Using Bronfenbrenner’s ecological framework to consider the effect of all spheres on these developing adolescents, I asked the question: How do Brazilian bi/multilingual adolescents in Southwest Florida use English and Portuguese across institutional contexts? Through ethnographic observation and semi-structured interviews, I found that due to language surveillance and the hegemony of English, Brazilian bi/multilingual adolescents use primarily English for communicating with those inside and outside their diasporic community. However, notably, Brazilian bi/multilingual adolescents use Portuguese to find belonging with other Brazilians, authenticate their uniqueness, and forge spaces where they belong and that uplift their identities –  an act I define as resilience and agency in light of their ecological context. In the face of institutional and systemic risk factors that question adolescents’ right to belong and flourish, the development of belonging and the uplifting of their identity within their community works as a protective factor promoting resilience in adolescents. My findings demonstrate how adolescents develop in contexts where their sense of belonging and identity are questioned, how bi/multilingual adolescents utilize language across contexts with different norms, and how minority diasporic communities use language for strength and resilience.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
